Here is an example of how you could implement atoi() in C:
```
#include <stdlib.h>

int atoi(const char *str) {
    int value = 0;
    for (int i = 0; str[i] != '\0'; i++) {
        if (!isdigit(str[i])) {
            return -1;
        }
        value = value * 10 + (str[i] - '0');
    }
    return value;
}
```
This function takes a null-terminated string as input and returns its integer representation. The function iterates through the characters in the string, checking each one to make sure it is a digit using the `isdigit()` function from the `ctype.h` header file. If any character in the string is not a digit, the function returns -1. Otherwise, the function multiplies the current value of `value` by 10 and adds the numerical value of the next character in the string to it.

To use this function, you can pass it a command line argument as follows:
```
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc > 1) {
        int value = atoi(argv[1]);
        printf("%d\n", value);
    }
    return 0;
}
```
This code will print the integer representation of the first command line argument to the console. For example, if the user runs `./a.out foo bar baz` and `foo` is the first argument, the program will print the integer representation of `foo`.']

