Here's a sample C code that does what you described:
```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc <= 1) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s [index]\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    int index = atoi(argv[1]);
    if (index < 1 || index > argc - 1) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Index out of range\n");
        return 1;
    }

    printf("%s\n", argv[index]);
    return 0;
}
```
Here's how the code works:

1. `int main(int argc, char *argv[])`: This is the standard function signature for C programs that receive command line arguments. The `argc` variable contains the number of command line arguments passed to the program, and the `argv` array contains the actual arguments as null-terminated strings.
2. `if (argc <= 1) { ... }`: This checks if there are no command line arguments passed to the program. If there are none, it prints an error message and exits with a non-zero status code (i.e., failure).
3. `int index = atoi(argv[1]);`: This converts the first command line argument (index) to an integer using the `atoi` function. The `atoi` function returns 0 if the conversion fails, so this also checks that the user has passed a valid index as a command line argument.
4. `if (index < 1 || index > argc - 1) { ... }`: This checks if the index is out of range. If it is, it prints an error message and exits with a non-zero status code (i.e., failure).
5. `printf("%s\n", argv[index]);`: This prints the command line argument at the specified index to the console. The `%s` format specifier tells `printf` to print a string, and the `\n` at the end of the format string adds a newline character at the end of the output.
6. `return 0;`: This returns a zero exit status code to indicate success.

To use this program, you can compile it with a C compiler (e.g., `gcc`) and then run it like any other executable: `./argv_index 1` will print the second command line argument passed to the program, while `./argv_index 3` will print the fourth command line argument passed to the program.']

